Yes — **`Backend` is the thing that should line up with `SandboxService`**.  
No — it should **not** be a protobuf *generated gRPC service client/server*.

Those are easy to mix up. Here’s the simple version.

### Two different “services”

| Thing | What it is | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Protobuf `SandboxService` | A **menu** of operations Kit supports | Create, Stop, Delete… |
| Your `Backend` / `BackendBase` | A **plug** each provider implements | DaytonaBackend, ModalBackend |

```text
App  →  Client/Sandbox (Kit facade)
           ↓
        Backend interface   ←── this matches the menu
           ↓
     DaytonaBackend / ModalBackend  ←── real vendor SDK calls
```

So your instinct is right: **Backend ≈ “implement SandboxService as local adapters.”**

What we did **not** do is: generate a gRPC `SandboxService` and make Backend call that.

---

### What `Backend` looks like today

TypeScript (one interface, optional methods):

```ts
export interface Backend {
  readonly name: string;
  create?(...): Promise<CreateResult>;
  stop?(...): Promise<SandboxInfo>;
  terminate?(...): Promise<ExitResult>;
  // ...
  close(...): Promise<void>;
}
```

Go (same idea, split into small interfaces):

```go
type Backend interface {
  Name() string
  Close(context.Context) error
}

type SandboxCreator interface {
  Create(context.Context, *CreateOptions) (*CreateResult, error)
}

type SandboxStopper interface {
  StopSandbox(context.Context, string, *StopOptions) (*SandboxInfo, error)
}
```

Protobuf already names those adapters:

```protobuf
rpc Create(...) {
  option (kit.codegen.v1.operation) = {
    adapter_interface: "sandbox_creator"
    adapter_method: "create"
  };
}
rpc Stop(...) {
  option (kit.codegen.v1.operation) = {
    adapter_interface: "sandbox_stopper"
    adapter_method: "stop_sandbox"
  };
}
```

So: **the proto already describes Backend.** We just wrote Backend by hand instead of generating it from those annotations.

---

### What `BackendBase` is

In Daytona/Modal it’s a tiny helper, not a proto service:

```ts
export abstract class BackendBase implements Backend {
  readonly name = providerName;
  abstract create(...): Promise<CreateResult>;
  abstract close(...): Promise<void>;
}
```

Plain English: “every Daytona backend must have a name, create, and close.”  
The comment even says it was meant as **shared generated** wiring — today it’s handwritten leftover shape.

`ModalBackend extends BackendBase` then fills in real Modal SDK calls. That’s the part protobuf can never write for you.

---

### Why not “use the proto-generated service” for Backend?

Because protobuf plugins generate the **wrong kind** of Backend:

```ts
// What Connect/gRPC codegen would give you (wrong for Kit)
class SandboxServiceClient {
  create(req) { return this.rpc("/kit.sandbox.v1.SandboxService/Create", req); }
}
```

That expects a Kit server on the network.

What Kit needs instead:

```ts
// What Backend actually is (right for Kit)
class DaytonaBackend {
  async create(request, options) {
    // map Kit fields → Daytona SDK
    return daytonaClient.create(...);
  }
}
```

Same **operation names**, totally different **implementation**.

---

### So should Backend come from proto?

**Yes, as generated TypeScript/Go interfaces** from `SandboxService` + `operation` options.

Example of the improvement (user API unchanged):

**Today (handwritten):**
```ts
export interface Backend {
  create?(...): ...
  stop?(...): ...
  // keep editing this when proto adds an RPC
}
```

**Better (generated from proto):**
```ts
// GENERATED from SandboxService — don’t edit
export interface Backend {
  readonly name: string;
  create?(...): Promise<CreateResult>;
  stop?(...): Promise<SandboxInfo>;
  // new rpc Archive → appears here automatically
  close(...): Promise<void>;
}
```

Providers still write:

```ts
class DaytonaBackend extends BackendBase {
  async create(...) { /* Daytona SDK */ }
  async stop(...) { /* Daytona SDK */ }
}
```

Apps still write:

```ts
await client.create({ ... });
await sandbox.stop({ timeout: 60_000 });
```

Nothing changes for users. You just stop hand-maintaining `Backend` / `SandboxCreator` / `SandboxStopper` so they can’t drift from the proto menu.

---

### One-line answer

**`Backend` / `BackendBase` should mirror protobuf `SandboxService` as local adapter interfaces — and those interfaces could be generated.**  
They should **not** be a protobuf-generated gRPC service, because Backend talks to Daytona/Modal, not to a Kit RPC server.

